package tuplestruct_ctor_declared_binder_name_fail;
struct P<'s>(&'s i64, &'s i64);
fn mk<'a, 'b>(x: &'a i64, y: &'b i64) -> P<'a> {
    return P(x, y);
}
fn main() -> i32 {
    let a: i64 = 2i64;
    let b: i64 = 5i64;
    let p: P = mk(&a, &b);
    return (*p.0 + *p.1) as i32;
}
// Was SOUNDNESS QUEUE row tuplestruct_ctor_declared_binder_name_admits (tier 2); closed by #465 (a by-value ADT region its definition uses only covariantly claims, as a plain `&'a` does).
// Illegal: `P(x, y)` puts a `&'b i64` into a `P<'a>` with no `'b: 'a` (rustc: lifetime may not live long enough).
// TODAY admitted. The tuple-struct constructor (lower_call's `B-ts-01` arm) instantiates NO binder: each field is
// compared by check_variance against the DECLARED `&'s i64`, and `'s` is not a binder of the fn under check, so
// outlives() answers by its permissive default. Rule 12's shape: with the binder spelled `'a` like the fn's, the
// same body is refused (c10, "tuple-struct 'P' field 1: variance mismatch — expected &'a i64, got &'b i64").
// Twin row: tuplestruct_ctor_declared_binder_name_refused. Found 2026-09-14c-meetobl (T1), base 47bea7f0aeb42d36;
// unchanged under mobl / moblany.
// rustc 1.98.1 --edition 2024 (round 2026-09-15f-rustcaudit, class CONFIRMED): REFUSED — error: lifetime may not live long enough. Twin src/compiler/probes/2026-09-15f-rustcaudit/rust/tuplestruct_ctor_declared_binder_name_admits.rs.
